+# Decision: Concurrency Mechanism for Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+A coastal conservation team records roughly 6,000 plot-visit records per season on 12 shared, intermittently offline tablets. A visit is a worker's coherent set of observations — observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes — tied to a stable catalog plot. Tablets can be offline for 48 hours, and two workers sometimes correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes, so edits can arrive days apart from the same starting state. The server must not treat arrival order as evidence that one worker's observations are more authoritative, and the HLD tenet *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* forbids losing one worker's submission to another's.
+
+How concurrent edits are reconciled is the load-bearing choice for this node. It fixes the submission envelope tablets must send, the shape of review work two coordinators perform, and which record states are eligible for the reviewed CSV the science partner receives every Friday. Getting it wrong either silently fuses observations whose scientific relationship the system cannot see, or produces more review work than two coordinators can clear before the export.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+### Gates
+
+- **Inherited — no submitted edit may be silently discarded.** Drawn from the HLD tenet *Do not silently discard a submitted edit*. An approach that lets one accepted submission's content disappear from the record without being retained and reviewable fails. This eliminates last-arrival-wins overwrites; the other options pass.
+
+### Weighted criteria
+
+- **Preservation of whole-visit scientific intent — major.** (Tenet: *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*; HLD Non-Goal: *Automated botanical validation or inference of scientific intent*.) A visit's fields can be scientifically related in ways field names do not reveal — a revised species identification and its cover estimate, for example. An approach that automatically joins edits judged "unrelated" by field name risks combining changes whose relationship no rule understands.
+- **Review workload two coordinators can clear before the Friday export — major.** (Tenet: *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*; Goal: *Make reviewable problems visible before the Friday export*.) Two coordinators handle all questions about submitted observations; the number and depth of items they must resolve is a standing cost.
+- **Ongoing merge-rule complexity — moderate.** Rules for combining scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries must be written and kept correct as the record schema changes. This is a local engineering cost with no named tenet.
+- **A worker's valid correction reaches the exported record — moderate.** When a worker's revision loses a whole-visit comparison, even a correct, unrelated change within it does not enter the exported value, and the worker may have to re-submit. This cuts against whole-visit revisions.
+- **Submission size and tablet storage — minor.** Whole-visit submissions carry a complete visit payload; structured-change submissions carry only deltas. At roughly 6,000 visits per season this is small but non-zero.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Each submission names the revision it started from (a base revision ID) and is stored as a complete, intact revision of the visit. When two or more accepted revisions share a base, the server holds them as competing revisions — unresolved review work — and combines nothing. A coordinator selects one revision as the visit's current state. All submissions remain in history; resolution retains the coordinator's identity, the chosen revision, and the contributing submission IDs. The server never infers which fields are "unrelated," so no field-combination logic has to be maintained.
+
+**Demonstration.** Workers A and B both fetch revision `r7` of visit `v`. A submits `r8` (corrects an observer name); B submits `r9` (adds a species). Both are retained as competing revisions from base `r7`, and the visit is unresolved until a coordinator selects one.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- *Gate — no silent discard*: **passes** — both `r8` and `r9` remain in history regardless of which is selected.
+- *Preservation of whole-visit scientific intent (major)*: **strong** — no fields are combined, so the system never joins edits whose relationship it cannot see.
+- *Review workload (major)*: **weak** — even edits to unrelated fields create a comparison item, so the queue grows with every concurrent edit.
+- *Ongoing merge-rule complexity (moderate)*: **strong** — there are no field-combination rules to write or maintain.
+- *A worker's valid correction reaches export (moderate)*: **weak** — the losing revision's correct changes do not enter the exported value.
+- *Submission size (minor)*: **weak** — each submission carries the full visit payload.
+
+**Summary.** Maximum preservation of submitted visits and minimal logic, at the cost of the largest review queue.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each submission supplies the base state it started from and a structured set of field-level changes. The server combines changes to disjoint fields automatically; changes that overlap the same field are flagged for coordinator review. Merge rules define combination for scalar fields (take the later value), observer lists (union), and species entries (per species). A combined record can be required to receive manual approval before export, and field-level provenance can record which submission supplied each value, so the exported value stays inspectable. Its appeal is real: disjoint edits are resolved without human work, so fewer visits wait for review before the Friday export, and a worker's valid unrelated correction is incorporated directly into the current record.
+
+**Demonstration.** From base `r7`, A changes the observer name and B adds a species; these are disjoint, so the server produces one current record containing both, with no review item. Had both changed the same species' cover estimate, that overlap would be queued for a coordinator.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- *Gate — no silent discard*: **passes** — original submissions are retained, and combined records can be held for review before export.
+- *Preservation of whole-visit scientific intent (major)*: **weak** — disjointness is judged from field names, so a change to a species identification and a change to its cover estimate can be combined as "unrelated" when they are scientifically related, with no reviewer noticing the pairing.
+- *Review workload (major)*: **strong** — only overlapping-field changes need review; unrelated edits never create work.
+- *Ongoing merge-rule complexity (moderate)*: **weak** — rules for scalars, lists, and species entries must be written and maintained as the schema evolves.
+- *A worker's valid correction reaches export (moderate)*: **strong** — a disjoint correction is incorporated directly into the current record.
+- *Submission size (minor)*: **strong** — structured deltas are smaller than full payloads.
+
+**Summary.** Lowest review volume, smallest submissions, and the most direct route from a valid correction to the export, at the cost of maintained merge rules and the risk of unnoticed cross-field combinations.
+
+### Last-arrival-wins overwrites
+
+**Description.** The most recently received submission replaces the visit's current state; earlier submissions are superseded.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- *Gate — no silent discard*: **eliminated** — a superseded submission's edits drop out of the current record with no review or retained alternative.
+
+**Summary.** Eliminated by the inherited gate; recorded only to make that boundary explicit.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Chosen: whole-visit revisions.**
+
+The gate eliminates last-arrival-wins. Between the two survivors, the decision turns on the major criteria, and the result is an explicit trade rather than a clean sweep: whole-visit revisions are **strong** on *preservation of whole-visit scientific intent* where field-aware merging is **weak**, because the HLD rules out inference of scientific intent and a merge that joins a species identification with its cover estimate as "disjoint" combines exactly the relationship the system cannot see. Whole-visit revisions are **weak** on *review workload two coordinators can clear before the Friday export* where field-aware merging is **strong** — every concurrent edit, even to unrelated fields, becomes a comparison item. That criterion is deliberately conceded.
+
+This decision turns on the tenets *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* and *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*, together with the Non-Goal *Automated botanical validation or inference of scientific intent*. It is taken **against** the tenet *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*: a larger review queue is accepted in preference to maintained merge rules that could combine scientifically related edits without a reviewer noticing. The moderate and minor costs — a losing worker's valid correction not reaching the export, full-payload submissions, and whole-visit comparisons — are accepted with it.
+
+Committing forecloses automatic combination of unrelated field edits. A visit with concurrent edits stays out of the reviewed CSV until a coordinator resolves it, so review volume and Friday export-readiness are worse than under field-aware merging; field-level provenance is not needed.
+
+**Why the field-aware approach stays in the domain, and when to re-open this choice.** Field-aware three-way merge remains credible because it is strong on exactly the criteria whole-visit revisions concedes: review volume, export throughput, submission size, and getting a valid correction into the exported record. Revisit this decision if the assumptions that make the larger review queue acceptable stop holding — if concurrent edits grow beyond what two coordinators can clear before the Friday export, if the partner needs more visits export-ready each week than review capacity allows, or if field workers routinely re-submit corrections that lose whole-visit comparisons. Revisit it also if a way emerges to bound merge rules so scientifically related edits cannot be combined without review (for example, a field-relationship model kept outside the merge logic), since that would neutralize the major criterion driving this choice.
+
+**Downstream obligations — cascades owned by the deferred nodes.** This choice fixes what tablet-sync and review-export must accommodate; neither is designed here.
+
+- **Tablet-sync** must retain base revision IDs in local drafts, submit complete visit payloads against a base revision rather than structured deltas, size its retry and idempotency state around whole-visit payloads, and show field workers the pending competing revisions so a worker can see when a submission is awaiting resolution. It does not need base snapshots or structured-change construction.
+- **Review-export** must provide a revision comparison view so two coordinators can compare competing complete revisions, and must hold visits carrying unresolved review work out of the reviewed CSV. It needs revision-level provenance — which submission produced the selected revision — not the field-level provenance a field-aware design would require.
+
+These are obligations the chosen mechanism places on sibling segments, not co-owned decisions. The substance of the choice lives in record-store; tablet-sync and review-export consume its submission envelope and unresolved-work representation when their designs are written.
