+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrency mechanism for independent edits to one visit ([decision doc](decisions/concurrency-mechanism.md)) | Whole-visit revisions: store each submission as an intact competing revision and let a coordinator select the current one | Field-aware three-way merge (combine disjoint field changes automatically, review overlaps); last-arrival-wins overwrites | Field-aware merging reduces review volume but requires maintained merge rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries, and can combine edits that are scientifically related without a reviewer noticing. A larger review queue is preferred over that risk, so even unrelated-field edits create review work. Whole-visit revisions preserve each submitted visit as the unit of scientific intent, keep the path from submission to export inspectable, and need no field-combination logic. Last-arrival-wins would silently discard a submitted edit and is excluded by the HLD tenet against discarding submissions. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+1. How the server treats a submission naming an unknown, stale, or already-superseded base revision ID.
+2. Whether a coordinator may resolve by authoring a new synthesized revision rather than selecting one of the submitted revisions.
+3. Behavior when a submission arrives during or after resolution of the same visit's earlier review work.
+4. The API error shape and status codes for rejected submissions (stale base, payload/key mismatch, invalid plot reference).
+5. Idempotency-key scope (per worker, per visit, or global) and collision behavior across workers.
+6. Whether a visit with more than two competing revisions is resolved by a single selection or a sequence.
